If $bold(X)$ is a Gaussian vector, $matbold(A)$ an $m times n$ matrix and $bold(b) in RR^m$, then $matbold(A) bold(X) + bold(b)$ is also a Gaussian vector.

For any $bold(u) in RR^n$, let $bold(v) = matbold(A)^tp bold(u)$; then
$
  bold(u)^tp (matbold(A) bold(X) + bold(b)) & = bold(v)^tp bold(X) + sum_(i=1)^n u_i b_i,
$
a Gaussian random variable plus a constant, hence a Gaussian random variable.
